NNOOTTEESS
       When TTY auditing is enabled, it is inherited by all processes started
       by that user. In particular, daemons restarted by a user will still
       have TTY auditing enabled, and audit TTY input even by other users
       unless auditing for these users is explicitly disabled. Therefore, it
       is recommended to use ddiissaabbllee==** as the first option for most daemons
       using PAM.

       To view the data that was logged by the kernel to audit use the command
       aauurreeppoorrtt ----ttttyy.

       The _p_a_t_t_e_r_n_s are comma separated lists of glob patterns or ranges of
       uids. A range is specified as _m_i_n___u_i_d:_m_a_x___u_i_d where one of these values
       can be empty. If _m_i_n___u_i_d is empty only user with the uid _m_a_x___u_i_d will
       be matched. If _m_a_x___u_i_d is empty users with the uid greater than or
       equal to _m_i_n___u_i_d will be matched.

       Please note that passwords in some circumstances may be logged by TTY
       auditing even if the lloogg__ppaasssswwdd is not used. For example, all input to
       an ssh session will be logged - even if there is a password being typed
       into some software running at the remote host because only the local
       TTY state affects the local TTY auditing.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
       Audit all administrative actions.

